Based on the latest component you shared, we'll make only the changes required for your shipment-alert story and preserve today's pulled changes, including Shipment Protection Claim, Void Shipment, and the existing alert modal.

There are three targeted changes:

* Update `bellTooltip` to show the unavailable-alert message.

* Add a getter to determine which bell icon to display.

* Update the HTML to display `notifications_off` and prevent interaction without using the unsupported `[disabled]` input on `OptiButtonComponent`.

### Step 1: Update the TypeScript component

In `ShipmentTrackingActionsCellRendererComponent`, add this constant near your existing component properties:

```typescript
private readonly ALERTS_UNAVAILABLE_TOOLTIP =
  'Alerts are not available for this shipment.';
```

Keep all existing properties and methods unchanged.

### Step 2: Add the bell icon getter

Add this getter immediately after your existing `enableBellIcon()` method:

```typescript

get bellIcon(): string {
  if (!this.enableBellIcon()) {
    return 'notifications_off';
  }

  return this.icontoggle
    ? 'notifications_active'
    : 'notifications_none';
}
```

This centralizes the icon selection and preserves the existing subscribed/unsubscribed behavior when alerts are available.

### Step 3: Update the `bellTooltip` getter

Find your existing `bellTooltip` getter, currently located after `normalizeValue()`. Replace only that getter with:

```typescript

get bellTooltip(): string {
  if (!this.enableBellIcon()) {
    return this.ALERTS_UNAVAILABLE_TOOLTIP;
  }

  if (this.notificationResource?.length) {
    const recipients = this.notificationResource
      .map(item =>
        this.phoneFormat.transform(item['communicationValue'])
      )
      .join('\n');

    return `(${this.notificationResource.length}) Alert Recipients\n${recipients}`;
  }

  return ACTIONS_SIDEBAR_MENU.ALERT_NOTIFY;
}
```

This retains the existing recipient information and manage-alerts tooltip for eligible shipments.

### Step 4: Update the HTML bell button

In `actions-sidebar-cell-renderer.component.html`, replace only the existing bell-button `@if` block with:

```html

@if (this.pageName === HEADER_CONSTANTS.TRACKING.route) {
  <span
    [matTooltip]="bellTooltip"
    matTooltipClass="multiline-tooltip"
    matTooltipPosition="above"
    style="display: inline-flex"
  >
    <opti-button
      [centerIcon]="bellIcon"
      buttonType="secondary"
      [class.alert-disabled]="!enableBellIcon()"
      (click)="onAlert()"
    ></opti-button>
  </span>
}
```

Why this avoids your previous error: We have removed `[disabled]` from `opti-button`, since Angular reported that it isn't a supported input. Instead, the conditional CSS class prevents mouse interaction when alerts are unavailable. Your existing `onAlert()` method also checks availability before opening the modal.

### Step 5: Update the SCSS

Open `actions-sidebar-cell-renderer.component.scss` and add:

```scss
.alert-disabled {
  pointer-events: none;
  opacity: 0.6;
}
```

The tooltip stays on the wrapper, so users can still hover over the unavailable bell and see the explanation.

Note that this CSS approach blocks mouse interaction but is not a fully accessible native disabled state. If `OptiButtonComponent` supports a disabled input under a different name, prefer that supported input.

